course: dictionary
lesson: 8406
title: Rumours and checking the facts
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A rumour is a story or piece of news that people pass from one to another. Nobody has checked whether it is true. «It is like a playground rumour that "school's closed tomorrow".» Before you believe it or pass it on, ask who said it and how they know.
explain: To be rumoured means people say something, but nobody is sure it is real. «It is rumoured that school will close.» The word rumoured tells you the news is still only talk, so look for a teacher or the school website to check.
explain: A rumour can be untrue, and even a true one can hurt. A good rule is to ask: is it true, is it kind, and is it necessary? If the answer is no, do not pass it on.
fact: A rumour is a story passed from person to person without anyone checking whether it is true.
fact: «A rumour can be untrue, and even a true one can hurt.»
fact: To be rumoured means people say it but nobody is sure.
fact: Phrases you may meet: spread a rumour, start a rumour, just a rumour, there is a rumour that.
fact: A clever reader sorts every story into four piles: what is confirmed, what is blown up, what is pure rumour, and what is simply private.
exercise: What is a rumour? | A song sung at assembly | A checked fact from a teacher | A rule about the playground | *A story passed from person to person that may not be true
exercise: «It is rumoured that school will close.» What does rumoured mean here? | *People say it, but nobody is sure | The school has already closed | Everyone has checked and it is certain | People are very cross
exercise: Before you pass on a rumour, which question helps most? | Is it longer than my last story? | *Is it true, kind and necessary? | Does it make my friends laugh? | Can I say it loudly?
test: Ava hears «school's closed tomorrow» from a Year 6 boy. What should she do first? | Believe it because he is older | Tell it as a joke | *Ask who said it, how they know, and check the school website | Pass it on to everyone at once
test: Which phrase means someone began a story that may be false? | Draw a map | *Start a rumour | Count to ten | Read a book
exam: «A rumour can be untrue, and even a true one can hurt.» What does this sentence teach us? | *Stories can hurt people whether or not they are true, so think before passing one on | Only untrue stories can hurt people | Rumours are always funny | True stories are always kind
